#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pass 53 - a counting question that was answered by semantic search.

    .venv/bin/python patches/quality_pass53.py            apply
    .venv/bin/python patches/quality_pass53.py --check    verify, change nothing

    "how many cars came into the shop this week?"

    The search returned four updates, but only one of them,
    [UPD-00001-08043], mentions a car coming into the shop...
    (As per the guidelines, I have not added any facts, figures, names
    or dates that are not in the passages.)

Four. The true answer is 179. Three separate faults put it there.

ROUTING. The keyword router knows "yesterday", "this morning" and every
weekday, and knows nothing about "this week". With no pattern matched the
planner fell through to its last resort, semantic search, which retrieved four
technician notes containing the word "shop" and handed them to an 8B. A count
over a window is not a search: arrival is a recorded column, `ros.checked_in_at`.

  - `get_intake(days)` counts what was booked in over a window and what became
    of it - still open, invoiced, blocked, open safety, waiters, the busiest day,
    the split by category. The eleventh tool, and the first new one since the
    shift-activity tool went in for the same reason: a question that WAS falling
    through to search.
  - `_window_days()` reads "this week", "this month", "the last 3 days",
    "today". Deliberately narrow: a window it cannot read is better answered
    over seven days with the window stated than over a window nobody meant.
  - The discriminator against `get_shift_activity` is the subject. "Who came in
    this morning" is people; "how many cars came in this week" is demand. "Came
    THROUGH" stays with shift activity, which is what it has always meant.

THE PROMPT. The narration prompt told the model what it must not do, and the
model wrote that down as part of the answer. Three rules were added: open with
the answer and never with what the search returned; never name the search, the
instructions, "the updates" or "the notes"; never make the same point twice.
The first draft of the fix said "write as one colleague to another", and the
next answer opened "Colleague, two Passat owners have reported..." - so it now
says no salutation and nothing addressed to the reader.

The figures block under a narrated answer carried exactly one number: "Updates
matching the search: 4". That is a fact about the retrieval, not about the shop,
and it is gone. The citation footer already names every source.

RETRIEVAL. The right repair order is inside the vector top-50 for every one of
the 40 probe queries, and the cross-encoder lifts it into the top 6 half the
time. So the candidates are not the problem and a wider pool was already
measured and rejected in pass 50. What was missing is the other signal: a
customer says "rattling noise on cold start", the technician writes "timing
chain tensioner replaced", and the words they DO share are the rare ones.
`_fuse` adds an IDF-weighted lexical ranking and combines it with the dense
ranking by reciprocal-rank fusion before the reranker sees anything.

Measured on two slices, the second never used to decide anything:

    repair orders 1-120     recall@6  50.0% -> 52.5%   MRR 0.251 -> 0.235
    repair orders 121-240   recall@6  51.7% -> 55.0%   MRR 0.204 -> 0.224

Recall improves on both. MRR is a wash. It is on by default and it still does
not reach the 60% floor.

One bug this nearly shipped: widening the pool for the no-rerank path made the
ablation return fifty passages where it should return six, and recall@6 for
"vector only" duly printed 100.0%. A measure that suddenly reads perfect is the
measure to distrust first.

THE MEASURES. Six intake questions joined the labelled routing set, so the new
class is scored rather than asserted: routing 24/24 -> 30/30, grounding 22/22
-> 28/28, traceability 524/524 -> 762/762, all still at 100%.
"""
